WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.810
Unit eight. Intro to Debugging!

2
00:00:04.813 --> 00:00:09.619
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emily, your technology teacher.

3
00:00:09.669 --> 00:00:12.708
Hi, Ms. Emily! What do we learn today?

4
00:00:12.758 --> 00:00:16.668
Today we learn about Intro to Debugging. Let's go!

5
00:00:18.058 --> 00:00:22.025
Debug. Say it with me. Debug!

6
00:00:25.537 --> 00:00:29.271
Bug. Say it with me. Bug!

7
00:00:32.802 --> 00:00:36.621
Error. Say it with me. Error!

8
00:00:40.092 --> 00:00:44.526
Fix code. Say it with me. Fix code!

9
00:00:48.021 --> 00:00:51.852
Test. Say it with me. Test!

10
00:00:55.314 --> 00:00:58.880
Trace. Say it with me. Trace!

11
00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:06.514
Problem. Say it with me. Problem!

12
00:01:10.007 --> 00:01:10.757
Logical error. Say it with me. Logical error!

13
00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:25.449
Debugging means finding and fixing a bug, or error, in code. I test and trace my program to find a logical error and fix it.

14
00:01:25.499 --> 00:01:32.067
Now I understand. Thank you, Ms. Emily!

15
00:01:33.068 --> 00:01:38.583
Look! What's the word?

16
00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:39.783
Error!

17
00:01:40.831 --> 00:01:46.176
Look! What's the word?

18
00:01:46.226 --> 00:01:47.376
Problem!

19
00:01:48.318 --> 00:02:00.293
Did you know? Engineers called problems 'bugs' long before computers, but a real moth found in a computer in 1947 made the story famous!

20
00:02:00.343 --> 00:02:02.840
Wow! I did not know that!

21
00:02:03.487 --> 00:02:14.716
Think and answer. Why do you think even experienced programmers still need to debug their code?

22
00:02:14.766 --> 00:02:16.000
Great answer!

23
00:02:16.857 --> 00:02:23.212
Great job today! Keep learning, and see you next time. Goodbye!

24
